20685	{"artifactId":"LLD-c2ba6fa62a148ed1-s4","entry":{"id":"migrated-LLD-c2ba6fa62a148ed1-s4-review","actor":{"id":"insrc:daemon","kind":"agent","properties":{}},"timestamp":"2026-10-11T08:42:31.181Z","operation":"review","outcome":{"result":"block","summary":"review: block","data":{"kind":"review","report":{"artifact":"design.story","stage":"design.story","verdict":"block","findings":[{"claimId":"coverage-of-intent.1","ref":"coverage-of-intent","item":"coverage-of-intent","kind":"semantic","severity":"LOW","premise":"The three acceptance criteria of Story s4 in the DEF (ac1 refusal of the live store, ac2 a stop that always ends, ac3 the true cause in status) are each designed, and ac1's remaining gap (the MCP server's process) is handed to Story s11 rather than dropped.","evidence":"DEF.md:217-219 states ac1-ac3 as the design reads them. The design covers ac1 by the owner check in getGraphStore, ac2 by the self-kill plus the forced kill in stopDaemon and daemon-ctl.sh, ac3 by StoreHealth and DaemonStatus.storeFailure. DEF.md:358 (s11 ac1) requires that 'the MCP server's process holds no descriptor on the live store', which matches the hand-over. src/mcp/server.ts:402 does run handleAnalyzeStep in the MCP process, so the temporary allowance is needed. Note: ac1 as written in the DEF is not met in full by this Story alone; the design says so openly. [files: docs/epics/complete-consistent-richer-code-index-one-E20261011c2ba6fa6/DEF.md, src/mcp/server.ts]","action":"None for the design. When approving, accept that ac1 is complete only after s11.","fixability":"manual","outcome":"holds"},{"claimId":"current-behaviour.1","ref":"current-behaviour","item":"current-behaviour","kind":"semantic","severity":"LOW","premise":"The daemon's shutdown ends in process.exit on both of its paths, with a 20-second backstop, and closes the store inside the queue-drain continuation.","evidence":"src/daemon/index.ts:1981 'const HARD_EXIT_MS = 20_000;'; the backstop at 2006-2010 calls clearPid() then process.exit(0); the drain path at 2012-2028 awaits closeGraphStore() (2022), clearPid(), then process.exit(0). The re-entrance guard 'shutdownStarted' is at 1986. daemon.shutdown (1575-1579) calls shutdown() and returns { ok: true }. [files: src/daemon/index.ts]","action":"None.","fixability":"manual","outcome":"holds"},{"claimId":"current-behaviour.2","ref":"current-behaviour","item":"current-behaviour","kind":"semantic","severity":"LOW","premise":"The stop paths give up after 30 seconds today, the request client has no time-out, and the status handler reads the repository list outside any guard.","evidence":"src/cli/services/daemon.ts:78-83: 'await rpc('daemon.shutdown'); if (!(await waitForStop())) { throw new Error('daemon did not stop within 30 s'); }'. src/shared/ipc-client.ts:46-84 has no timer; it settles only on data or on a socket error. scripts/daemon-ctl.sh:220 and :235 both 'die ... 4' after wait_for_daemon_stop. src/daemon/index.ts:1026 'repos: await listRepos(db),' sits outside the only try block of the handler (1036-1041). restart (maintenance.ts:276-288) returns ok:false with steps ['stop'] when stopDaemon throws. [files: src/cli/services/daemon.ts, src/shared/ipc-client.ts, scripts/daemon-ctl.sh, src/daemon/index.ts, src/cli/services/maintenance.ts]","action":"None. One detail for the build: rpc() maps both ENOENT and ECONNREFUSED to the same 'daemon is not running' message (ipc-client.ts:77-78), so stopDaemon must tell the two cases apart by the pid (pidFromFile / isRunning in src/cli/services/lifecycle.ts), not by the error text.","fixability":"manual","outcome":"holds"},{"claimId":"current-behaviour.3","ref":"current-behaviour","item":"current-behaviour","kind":"semantic","severity":"LOW","premise":"getGraphStore opens whatever path is set, defaulting to the live store; nothing handles the lock failure; most writes go through withWriteTxn; 80 test files set a temporary path and the two named test files do not.","evidence":"src/db/graph/store.ts:265 'let _path: string = PATHS.lmdb;', 280-430 has no ownership or health check, and closeGraphStore (606-616) always awaits inst.root.close(). withWriteTxn is 'store.root.transaction(() => fn(store))' (631) and withWriteTxnSync is 'store.root.transactionSync(fn)' (643), neither with a catch. A search of non-test source finds 50 withWriteTxn call sites in 9 files and no withWriteTxnSync caller outside store.ts. setGraphStorePath appears in exactly 80 test files; freeform-probe-scope.test.ts and phase5-explorations-params.test.ts have no call to it (the latter's line 77 comment says the read goes to listEntitiesForRepo). [files: src/db/graph/store.ts, src/db/graph/migrations.ts, src/analyze/explore/__tests__/freeform-probe-scope.test.ts, src/analyze/explore/__tests__/phase5-explorations-params.test.ts]","action":"Note for the build: two asynchronous transactions bypass the wrapper and so get no bounded wait: store.ts:386 (first-boot write) and src/db/graph/migrations.ts:442. Both run during open, before the daemon serves, so the risk is small; say so in the design or route them through the bounded wrapper.","fixability":"manual","outcome":"holds"},{"claimId":"new-versus-reuse.1","ref":"new-versus-reuse","item":"new-versus-reuse","kind":"semantic","severity":"LOW","premise":"The new names do not exist yet, and the reused pieces exist with the stated shape: LmdbStoreError as a base class, an injectable reader on resolveSourceRoots, and the pid helpers the stop needs.","evidence":"src/db/graph/store.ts:136 'export class LmdbStoreError extends Error' with four existing subclasses; the file has no declareLiveStoreOwner, StoreHealth, LmdbStoreNotOwnerError or LmdbStoreLocksLostError. src/workflow/review/source-roots.ts:47-54 already has 'SourceRootsOpts { readonly listEntities?: (repoPath: string) => Promise<readonly Entity[]> }' and returns fallbackUsed. src/cli/services/lifecycle.ts exports pidFromFile, isRunning and waitForStop. src/cli/services/daemon.ts:26 exports DAEMON_ENTRY as the design says, and both launchers put the entry last on the command line (daemon.ts:65, daemon-ctl.sh:138), so the 'ends in daemon/index.js' rule matches both. [files: src/db/graph/store.ts, src/workflow/review/source-roots.ts, src/cli/services/lifecycle.ts, src/cli/services/daemon.ts]","action":"None. The feedback note at the end of the design ('The pid check reuses DAEMON_ENTRY') contradicts section 2.4, which says it does not; delete the stale sentence.","fixability":"manual","outcome":"holds"},{"claimId":"change-sites.1","ref":"change-sites","item":"change-sites","kind":"semantic","severity":"HIGH","premise":"A refusal placed in resolvePath (src/daemon/tools/builtins/file/helpers.ts) covers every file tool, including copy and move.","evidence":"file_copy and file_move never call resolvePath when they run. copy.ts:8 imports only 'str, fail, FS_MOVE_ACCESS' and its execute does 'const src = resolve(from); const dst = resolve(to);' (57-58) then 'await fs.cp(src, dst, ...)' (75); move.ts:56-57 does the same and falls back to fs.cp at 79. Their only link to resolvePath is FS_MOVE_ACCESS.extractKey (helpers.ts:59-64), and a search of src for 'extractKey' finds no caller at all: only the declarations in the tools and the interface in src/shared/access.ts. So with the refusal built where the design puts it, file_copy still reads the lock file in the daemon's process, which the design itself names as the one known way to drop the lock. fs.cp is also not among the functions the diagnostic wraps, so the copy would not be logged either. The design's own test ('checked through the shared path helper for ... copy ... and move') would pass against the helper while the two tools stay open. [files: src/daemon/tools/builtins/file/copy.ts, src/daemon/tools/builtins/file/move.ts, src/daemon/tools/builtins/file/helpers.ts, src/shared/access.ts]","action":"Put the refusal in a helper that copy.ts and move.ts call in execute (for both 'from' and 'to') and in buildApprovalGate, or make the two tools use resolvePath. Add fs.cp and its promise form to the wrapped functions. Test the refusal by calling each tool's execute, not the helper. Also decide whether the check resolves symbolic links: resolvePath uses path.resolve, which does not.","fixability":"manual","outcome":"does-not-hold"},{"claimId":"change-sites.2","ref":"change-sites","item":"change-sites","kind":"semantic","severity":"MED","premise":"The shutdown's two ends are the only places where the daemon calls process.exit after its store has failed, so changing them establishes the invariant 'once locks-lost, the daemon never calls process.exit'.","evidence":"src/daemon/index.ts:49-65: reportFatal, registered for both 'uncaughtException' and 'unhandledRejection', ends with 'setTimeout(() => { process.exit(1); }, 50).unref();' (line 61). The design makes the write wrapper and getGraphStore reject with LmdbStoreLocksLostError; any such rejection that is not awaited (the shutdown itself uses 'void disposeChatHandlers()', 'void watcher.close()', and timers call asynchronous code) reaches this handler, which calls process.exit, whose exit hook the design says never returns. The daemon is then hung with its pid file in place and health recorded, which is the state the invariant exists to prevent. The section lists only the shutdown as a call site. [files: src/daemon/index.ts]","action":"Add reportFatal to section 3.4: when StoreHealth is locks-lost it removes the pid file and socket, writes its line synchronously (it already does, lines 52-56) and ends by SIGKILL to itself. Add a test: an unhandled rejection after locks-lost ends the process.","fixability":"manual","outcome":"does-not-hold"},{"claimId":"change-sites.3","ref":"change-sites","item":"change-sites","kind":"semantic","severity":"LOW","premise":"The change of stopDaemon's return type reaches the service type and exactly three callers.","evidence":"A search for stopDaemon finds: the definition (src/cli/services/daemon.ts:78), the service type 'stopDaemon(): Promise<void>;' (src/cli/services/index.ts:39) and its binding (:92), and the callers src/cli/command.ts:84, src/cli/panes/DaemonPane.tsx:53 and src/cli/services/maintenance.ts:279. No plugin calls it. [files: src/cli/services/index.ts, src/cli/command.ts, src/cli/panes/DaemonPane.tsx, src/cli/services/maintenance.ts, src/cli/__tests__/command.test.ts, src/cli/__tests__/tui.test.ts]","action":"Two test doubles also declare it and are not in the design: src/cli/__tests__/command.test.ts:34 and src/cli/__tests__/tui.test.ts:56 ('stopDaemon: async () => {}'). Update them in the same change; tsc does not check test files here, so nothing will flag them.","fixability":"manual","outcome":"holds"},{"claimId":"change-sites.4","ref":"change-sites","item":"change-sites","kind":"semantic","severity":"MED","premise":"The daemon's request dispatcher and its queue's job runner are the two places every store-using call passes through, so catching the read failure there records it.","evidence":"src/daemon/server.ts has three separate catch sites, not one: the standard request (177-183), the standard handler reached through a stream request (192-198), and the stream handler (226-233). All three reduce the error to its message ('const msg = err instanceof Error ? err.message : String(err)'), so the code must be read before that line at each. The queue runner is one site (src/daemon/queue.ts:178-181, 'log.error({ err }, 'job failed')'). Store reads also run outside both: the timers in src/daemon/index.ts (readerCheckTimer at 1959, pruneTimer) and the boot sequence (263-273). And a handler that swallows its own failures never lets the error reach the dispatcher: resolveSourceRoots (src/workflow/review/source-roots.ts:93-99) catches every read failure and falls back. In those cases the failure is recorded only by a later write's 10-second probe. [files: src/daemon/server.ts, src/daemon/queue.ts, src/daemon/index.ts, src/workflow/review/source-roots.ts]","action":"Name the three catch sites in server.ts in the design. State that a read outside the dispatcher and the queue, or inside a handler that swallows errors, is detected by the next write or the next dispatched read, and that this is accepted. Consider one cheap alternative: have the periodic reader check (already every 5 minutes) do the probe read and record the failure.","fixability":"manual","outcome":"does-not-hold"},{"claimId":"change-sites.5","ref":"change-sites","item":"change-sites","kind":"semantic","severity":"MED","premise":"The CLI's in-process review can be given an injected source-root reader by changing the CLI's call alone.","evidence":"The reader cannot be passed from the CLI today. src/cli/services/workflow.ts:248 calls reviewArtifactFile({ mdPath, jsonPath, repo, provider, model, reviewedAt }); the chain is reviewArtifactFile (src/workflow/review/run-artifact.ts:69) -> review (src/workflow/review/review.ts:85 'await gatherEvidence(claims, repo)') -> gatherEvidence(claims: Claim[], repoPath: string) (probe.ts:190) -> 'await resolveSourceRoots(repoPath)' with no options (probe.ts:195). Three signatures between the CLI and the seam need a new optional parameter, and the same functions run in the daemon (src/daemon/workflow-review-rpc.ts:100) and in the MCP server's review step, where the default reader must stay. None of these files, nor the new daemon request and its handler, appears in any call-site list of the design. The claim about the read path itself is true: source-roots.ts:57-60 reads through getDb and listEntitiesForRepo, and no file under src/cli or src/bin imports the store layer directly. [files: src/cli/services/workflow.ts, src/workflow/review/run-artifact.ts, src/workflow/review/review.ts, src/workflow/review/probe.ts, src/daemon/workflow-review-rpc.ts]","action":"Add a contract entry for the threaded option (ReviewArtifactOpts, the review options, gatherEvidence) and for the new read-only daemon request with its name, parameters and result. State that the option is optional so the daemon and MCP callers are unchanged.","fixability":"manual","outcome":"does-not-hold"},{"claimId":"data-compatibility.1","ref":"data-compatibility","item":"data-compatibility","kind":"semantic","severity":"LOW","premise":"No stored shape changes; DaemonStatus gains one optional field that existing consumers ignore, and the store's default path follows the home directory so a test with a temporary home never touches the real store.","evidence":"The status object is built in one place (src/daemon/index.ts:1024-1034) with spread-in optional fields already, so an absent field is the existing pattern. The VS Code side takes the type from src/shared/ipc-client.ts:14 ('export type { ... DaemonStatus } from './types.js''), so it has no second copy; JetBrains reads status in DaemonGateway.kt. src/shared/paths.ts:4 'const INSRC_DIR = join(homedir(), '.insrc');' is evaluated once at import, and store.ts:265 copies PATHS.lmdb at import. [files: src/daemon/index.ts, src/shared/ipc-client.ts, src/shared/paths.ts, src/db/graph/store.ts]","action":"Two build notes. (1) Because PATHS is fixed at import, a test that 'points the home directory at a temporary one' must set HOME before the first import of the store (a child process, or a dynamic import after setting it); say so in the fixtures. (2) The repository's convention is explicit '| undefined' on optional properties (exactOptionalPropertyTypes); write 'storeFailure?: {...} | undefined'.","fixability":"manual","outcome":"holds"},{"claimId":"boundaries.1","ref":"boundaries","item":"boundaries","kind":"semantic","severity":"LOW","premise":"The design does not redesign what Story s11 or another Story owns: it leaves the MCP server's reads in place and only adds a temporary allowance that s11 deletes.","evidence":"DEF.md:358-360 gives s11 the move of every step tool into the daemon and the criterion that the MCP process holds no descriptor on the live store. The design keeps src/mcp/analyze-step/phases/narrow.ts:30 ('import { putCachedExploration } from '../../../db/exploration-cache.js'') and handleAnalyzeStep (src/mcp/server.ts:402) as they are. The refusal it adds to the search tools is about two named files, not the ignore lists that Story s6 says it leaves alone in those tools, so the two do not collide. The by-file index (s3), IndexState (s1) and the file commit (s2) are not touched. [files: docs/epics/complete-consistent-richer-code-index-one-E20261011c2ba6fa6/DEF.md, src/mcp/server.ts, src/mcp/analyze-step/phases/narrow.ts]","action":"None.","fixability":"manual","outcome":"holds"},{"claimId":"error-paths.1","ref":"error-paths","item":"error-paths","kind":"semantic","severity":"LOW","premise":"A daemon that knows its store has failed stops within its normal stop time: the shutdown reads StoreHealth, skips the close, clears the pid file and socket and kills itself.","evidence":"The self-kill sits after the queue drain in today's structure: the close and the exit are inside 'void queueDone.finally(async () => {...})' (src/daemon/index.ts:2012-2028), and queue.stop() (src/daemon/queue.ts:189-192) only stops the loop 'after the current job finishes'. The design does not say whether, with locks-lost, the daemon waits for queueDone or ends at once. If it waits, a job hung in a store call ends only at the 20-second backstop (and the design's test expects an exit within 5 seconds); the summary says 'ends itself at once', section 3.4 says it 'stops the queue ... as today'. I could not settle which is meant from the text, and I did not run a daemon with broken locks. [files: src/daemon/index.ts, src/daemon/queue.ts]","action":"State in section 3.4 that with locks-lost the shutdown does not wait for queueDone: it runs the clean-up and the self-kill directly from shutdown(). Keep the 5-second test as the proof.","fixability":"manual","outcome":"could-not-verify"},{"claimId":"error-paths.2","ref":"error-paths","item":"error-paths","kind":"semantic","severity":"LOW","premise":"The three measured behaviours the detection rests on (a read throws code -30775, a synchronous transaction throws code 22, an asynchronous transaction never settles) and the claim that process.exit and the library's close hang once the locks are gone.","evidence":"These come from the design's own measurement of 2026-10-11 (reference c13). The source confirms only that nothing in src handles them: store.ts has no catch around transaction or transactionSync and classifyOpenError (669-695) covers open failures only. I did not run the two-process sequence against the installed lmdb-js, so the codes, and the choice of code 22 as a lock-failure signal, are unconfirmed here. Code 22 is the general 'Invalid argument'; treating every code-22 failure of a synchronous transaction as locks-lost could record a terminal failure for another cause. [files: src/db/graph/store.ts]","action":"Keep the reproduction test as the first thing built, as the design plans, and have withWriteTxnSync confirm a code-22 failure with the same probe read before it records locks-lost, since the record cannot be cleared without a restart.","fixability":"manual","outcome":"could-not-verify"},{"claimId":"tests.1","ref":"tests","item":"tests","kind":"semantic","severity":"LOW","premise":"Each of ac1, ac2 and ac3 maps to at least one test that would fail if the criterion were not met.","evidence":"The acceptance mapping names, for ac1, the two-process refusal test and the owner-check unit tests; for ac2, the store-failure-to-stop test, the forced stop through stopDaemon, and the control script's stop and start; for ac3, the status-after-failure test (cause names another process, no 'semume') and the detection tests through the real wrappers. The repository's suites are node:test files under __tests__ run with tsx, as the design says. The control-script tests need an override for the 30-second wait, which does not exist today: wait_for_daemon_stop hard-codes 'local deadline=$(( $(date +%s) + 30 ))' (scripts/daemon-ctl.sh:157), and the script reads the pid file and socket from $HOME, so the test must run it with a temporary HOME. [files: scripts/daemon-ctl.sh]","action":"Add the wait override to the script's change list in section 2.5. Add the tests asked for above: file_copy and file_move refused through their execute, and an unhandled rejection after locks-lost. Update the two CLI test doubles of stopDaemon.","fixability":"manual","outcome":"holds"}],"counts":{"high":1,"med":3,"low":11,"unverified":2},"template":"design-spec","reviewedAt":"2026-10-11T08:42:31.181Z","model":"cli-claude:opus","reviewedBy":"daemon"},"resolutions":{}}}}}
